  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 306 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nThis boundary maintains a close influence on the weather we \nexperience across the area most of the week and leads to a fairly \nuncertain forecast. Along and to the north of this boundary will \nfeature abundant precipitation leading to afternoon highs in the 70s, \nwith anomalously hot/near record temperatures to the south. \nCurrently, it appears that far northeast portions of MO and points \nto the north have the best potential to experience showers and \nstorms while also maintaining slightly cooler temperatures from \nWednesday into late week. Along/near this boundary there will be \nrich column moisture (PWATs near 2\") and a deep warm cloud layer \nsupporting warm rainfall processes and efficient rainfall \nproduction. If northeast MO/west-central IL receive multiple rounds \nof precipitation, something that remains uncertain, then heavy \nrainfall may become a concern. Currently, the greatest chance for \nheavy rainfall and flash flooding appears to remain across \nIowa/western MO through Wednesday. Beyond that, uncertainty \nincreases due to lack of consensus with guidance on where the \neffective boundary ends up at later in the week. Temperature IQRs \nfor most of the area indicate unseasonably hot conditions may \ncontinue into the weekend with spreads around 5-10 degrees centered \non temperatures near 10-15 degrees above average. By Sunday, IQR \nspreads increase to 15-20 degrees due to cooler 25th percentiles, \nindicating the potential is there for the return of cooler \ntemperatures. It is worth noting that even the 25th percentiles for \nSunday only return temperatures back to near average, illustrating \njust how anomalously hot temperatures will be this week.",
